[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its reports say otherwise. The ticket
holds three problems with three different fixes, and the queued parser fix closes only one of
them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not known. Three problems, one fix queued. Each now has a ticket of its own.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

Every count is built from `reports-4471.json` at render time, one report per id: the export
repeats some reports across its overlapping pages.

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** Our tenants all write the day
first. Their dates come out with day and month swapped: booked for 2 December, invite for
12 February. It reaches every channel: app, email, and the booking-v2 form. This is the bug
#4471 names, and the fix that is queued.

**`hint`: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.** People type what the hint tells
them, month first, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The result looks the same
as `parser`, so it was merged, but it only happens on the form, only for tenants on booking-v3,
and the parser fix does not touch it. The fix is the hint (or the form's reading), not the
parser.

**`shift`: a date moves by one day.** Not a swap. "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in
Toronto got Monday." "Every booking in June is one day off." An invite that arrived before the
booking. The desk already read these as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`), but the cause table
on #4471 still counts them under `parser` by tenant. It looks like a time-zone or DST conversion,
and no fix for it is queued.

## How to tell them apart

1. Does the report give two dates that are each other with day and month swapped? If not, and
   the date moved by a day, it is `shift`.
2. A swap that came in through the booking form of a booking-v3 tenant is `hint`.
3. Every other swap from a day-first tenant is `parser`.

Ask the tenant, not intake. Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser, and on #4471 it
disagrees with the tenant in both directions; the table shows by how much.

## What went wrong

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject, and every one of these
reports says "wrong date" in some form. Same symptom, different causes. The
[cause table](/kb/incidents/4471) already computes `hint` apart from `parser` and marks it
"no ticket of its own"; the page above it still says one issue.

